      <li><a href="https://aikidojournal.com/2005/04/07/takemusu-aiki-lectures-of-morihei-ueshiba-founder-of-aikido-1/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aikido Journal — Takemusu Aiki Lectures of Morihei Ueshiba</a> — the primary source of Ueshiba's talks: aikido as a universal principle, takemusu.</li>
      <li><a href="https://shugyokai.org/shugyo/aikido/ueshiba-morihei-doka/doka-135-musubi-of-true-void-emptiness/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Shugyokai — Dōka 135: Musubi of True Void &amp; Emptiness</a> — “musubi of true void and sky-void”; the double play of 空 = <em>kū</em>/<em>sora</em>.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.aikidosangenkai.org/blog/aikido-without-peace-harmony/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aikido Sangenkai — C. Li: Aikido without Peace or Harmony</a> — Takemusu Aiki; Saito on techniques “being born by themselves.”</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.theschooloflife.com/article/wu-wei-doing-nothing/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The School of Life — Wu Wei, Doing Nothing</a> — bamboo, water, the painter Zhang Zao; wu-wei in politics and business.</li>
      <li><a href="https://budobum.blogspot.com/2015/02/states-of-mind-mushin.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Budo Bum — States of Mind: Mushin (2015)</a> — Takuan on fudōshin; quieting the mind; technique without thinking.</li>
      <li><a href="https://www.itsuo-tsuda-school.org/sujet/aikido-2/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Itsuo Tsuda School — Aikido</a> — breath practice, non-doing, <em>ki</em> as the motor of instinctive manifestations.</li>
      <li><a href="https://romajidesu.com/dictionary/meaning-of-%E7%84%A1%E7%82%BA%E8%87%AA%E7%84%B6.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">RomajiDesu — 無為自然</a> — the dictionary definition: abandoning artifice.</li>
      <li><a href="https://omlc.org/aikido/talk/osensei/artofpeace/64.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Morihei Ueshiba, <em>The Art of Peace</em> no. 64 (trans. John Stevens)</a> — “Spring forth from the Great Earth; Billow like Great Waves; Stand like a tree, sit like a rock; Use One to strike All. Learn and forget!”</li>
      <li><a href="https://aikidojournal.com/2003/02/25/an-aikido-life-03/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Aikido Journal — Gozo Shioda: An Aikido Life, part III (2003)</a> — “Sensei often told us to learn techniques and forget them… you learn martial arts not with the brain but with the body”; confirmation of the sense: absorb with the body, then release.</li>
